    "text": "\n000\nFXUS65 KPSR 131711\nAFDPSR\n\nArea Forecast Discussion\nNational Weather Service Phoenix AZ\n1010 AM MST Sun Sep 13 2026\n\n.UPDATE...\nUpdated Aviation\n\n&&\n\n.KEY MESSAGES...\n\n- Abundant moisture will remain situated across the eastern half of \nArizona providing enhanced chances for showers and thunderstorms \nthrough the middle part of the week, especially over mountain \nlocations.\n\n- Although uncertainty remains, there will be potential for some \nstorms to produce strong winds and heavy rainfall over the next \nseveral days.\n\n- Near to slightly above normal temperatures are expected throughout \nweek.\n \n&&\n\n.SHORT TERM /Today through Monday/...\nThe main forecast concern early this week (and even through the \nmiddle of the week) will continue to be the conditional \nprobabilities of thunderstorms across the area, some of which could \nproduce localized strong winds, blowing dust, and heavy rainfall. \nThe subtropical ridge continues to give way to broad upper level \ntroughing building in across the Northwestern U.S. By Monday, the \nridge center is forecast to be positioned over the Southeastern U.S. \nwith the broad troughing filtering more into our region and likely \nstaying there for much of the week. At least the eastern half of \nArizona will remain well positioned much of this week for periodic \ninfluxes of moisture out of northern Mexico and off the Gulf of \nCalifornia. Moisture levels are expected to remain very adequate for \nshower and thunderstorm potential over the next several days with \nthe main limiting factors being the overall weak upper level support \nand weak to moderate convective inhibition.\n\nSaturday's showers and storms overachieved compared to forecast \nguidance, just like Friday. Models have had a difficult time with \ndepicting storm potential in this somewhat conditional environment \nand under a southwesterly steering flow. Unfortunately, this is \nlikely to be the case for today and probably most days for the \ncoming week. Our expectations is for today and Monday to at least \nhave a 2 or 3 periods of showers and isolated thunderstorms with the \nCAMs favoring late this afternoon/early evening, Monday morning and \nagain later Monday. We should have plenty of insolation today \nresulting in modestly high CAPE of 1000-1500 J/kg with little CIN \nlater in the afternoon into the evening. Although CAMs really can't \nagree on convection for today, it is logical for storms (some \npotentially strong) to develop with a focus over higher terrain \nareas. Chances into the south-central Arizona lower deserts are \nexpected to be more limited, but PoPs have been adjusted upward to \nat least include 10-15% chances for eastern and northern portions of \nthe Phoenix metro. \n\nAfter a brief reprieve in rain chances later this evening/early \ntonight, a couple HREF members are pointing at a round of elevated \nshowers and embedded thunderstorms at some point between 3 AM-10 AM \nMonday. The more likely time frame for this type of convection \nshould be a couple hours before sunrise before gradually diminishing \nshortly after sunrise. Given the expected elevated nature of these \npotential showers and storms, we should only expect localized heavy \nrainfall as the only threat. For the latter half of Monday, the best \npotential for showers and storms should be over southeast Arizona \nand potentially into the higher terrain east of Phoenix. Given the \nforecast CAPE values of up to around 1500 J/kg and very respectable \nlow level mixing ratios of 13-14 g/kg, this should present a heavy \nrainfall and localized flood threat into southern Gila County later \nMonday. \n\n&&\n\n.LONG TERM /Tuesday through Saturday/...\nThe weather pattern through at least the middle of the week will \ncontinue to support good moisture levels over at least the eastern \nhalf of Arizona and likely improved upper level ascent for Tuesday-\nThursday. Tuesday may involve a brief period of drier air moving \ninto south-central Arizona from the southwest and this shows up in \nthe fairly limited PoPs Tuesday afternoon/evening. However, NBM \nguidance shows a resurgence of moisture and rain chances during the \novernight hours Tuesday night/Wednesday morning. Chances Wednesday \ninto Thursday are also looking better with a potential disturbance \ntracking northward into central/eastern Arizona at some point. \nNeedless to say, the model differences call into question the best \ntiming for rain chances and the exact areas, but as long as the \nupper level support and good moisture remain present chances will \nremain elevated. \n\nWPC has added a slight risk on the Days 2-4 (Monday- Wednesday) \nExcessive Rainfall Outlooks over the eastern Arizona high terrain. \nIt would not be surprising for subsequent issuance's of the EROs to \nextend the risk through Thursday or even Friday depending on the \nforecast available moisture. Although the risk for heavy rainfall \nand flooding will be present through at least the middle of the \nweek, the risk for strong downdraft/outflow winds is likely to \ndecrease with guidance showing decreasing forecast DCAPEs. Models \nare still trending toward drier air finally moving eastward through \nmost of Arizona by Friday or Saturday. This should mostly shut down \nrain chances over the lower deserts and decrease chances over higher \nterrain areas.\n\nTemperatures throughout the entire week are expected to remain \nfairly close to seasonal normals with the lowest forecast highs \noccurring on Tuesday and Wednesday. As long as models are correct \nwith the drying trend late in the week into next weekend, \ntemperatures are favored to drift back to above normal with highs \nmore in a 103-108 degree range by Friday or Saturday.\n\n&&\n\n.AVIATION...Updated at 1710Z. \n\nSouth Central Arizona including KPHX, KIWA, KSDL, and KDVT: \nVery low confidence forecast through Monday given vast uncertainty \nin thunderstorm development/coverage and resulting effects on winds \nand flight categories. Confidence is good that an extended period of \nsoutherly cross runway winds will exist early this afternoon before \nbecoming W/SW by late afternoon. Similar to yesterday, TSRA may \ndevelop over terrain features with modest outflow winds and isold \nBLDU around the airspace, but not directly impacting terminals \n(chances less than 25%). However, its not out of the question for \nwesterly winds to be disrupted at some point this evening by effect \nfrom storms. \n\nModels still want to blossom SHRA/TSRA over/near the Phoenix metro \nMonday morning around sunrise, though confidence in location and \ncoverage is low, and only justifying a Prob30 mention at this time. \nIf SHRA/TSRA indeed materialize, erratic wind directions and \npotentially briefly lower flight categories will be possible through \nmid/late morning. Trends in winds and additional convective \ndevelopment the remainder of Monday will be highly contingent on \nmorning activity (or lack there of). \n\n\nSoutheast California/Southwest Arizona including KIPL and KBLH: \nNo weather concerns will exist through Monday afternoon under mostly \nclear skies. Confidence is very good that winds will behave similar \nto the past 24 hours varying between a SE and SW heading. Afternoon \ngusts around 20kt will be common at KBLH. \n\n&&\n\n.FIRE WEATHER...\nChances for scattered showers and isolated storms will continue \ntoday before increasing and becoming more widespread into the south-\ncentral Arizona lower deserts beginning Monday. Moisture levels will \nremain high enough to support localized heavy rainfall with any \nstorms. Minimum humidity levels over the next several days will only \nfall into a 15-25% range in western districts and 25-40% in eastern \ndistricts. This will follow fair to good overnight recovery of 40-\n70%. Outside of thunderstorms, winds will generally remain light \nwith a southwest heading through nearly calm conditions will be \ncommon in the morning hours. \n\n&&\n\n.PSR WATCHES/WARNINGS/ADVISORIES...\nAZ...None.\nCA...None.\n\n&&\n\n$$\n\nSHORT TERM...Kuhlman\nLONG TERM...Kuhlman\nAVIATION...18\nFIRE WEATHER...Kuhlman\n",
